# Drain policy for the learned-summary staleness ceiling.
#
# Read by check_drain (scripts/dev/learned_staleness.py) on every run of that gate,
# which is every `make ze-doc-test` inside `make ze-verify`.
# Owned by ai/rules/planning.md, "Writing Learned Summaries", and ultimately by Thomas.
#
# THIS FILE CARRIES POLICY ONLY: a start date and a rate. Two fields, nothing else.
# It may NEVER gain a per-summary row, a count, a file list, or a rule column. The
# count lives in plan/.learned-staleness-baseline, it is derived from a run, and one
# flag writes it. A second copy here would drift from that one, and a reviewer should
# treat such a line as a defect rather than an extension.
#
#   start  YYYY-MM-DD the drain clock begins counting from.
#   rate   Dead references the corpus must have REPAIRED per calendar month. The
#          required drain is ceil(rate x whole calendar months since start), CAPPED at
#          the ceiling in force when the clock started. Progress is measured from that
#          same anchor, so tightening the baseline never raises the bar the corpus is
#          measured against. The check also retires itself: a corpus at zero dead
#          references has drained the whole anchor, and the floor can never exceed it.
#
# The rate SHIPS AT 0, so the comparison lands inert and nothing changes today. Arming
# it is a one-line commit that sets a non-zero rate, and Thomas takes it. Guessing a
# rate now would either demand nothing or red the tree on unrelated work.
#
# The mechanism exists so the answer to "this tax is permanent" is "arm the drain",
# never "delete the gate" (ai/rules/planning.md, "The learned-staleness ceiling is
# drained, never removed and never widened").

start 2026-08-07
rate 0
